     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="FR">10 new data sources per year 

Regarding new data sources, mainly 2 new data sources have been exploited, i.e. web analytics and app data analysis in the cases of 1) the Healthy Internet Project where a browser extension was tested for its capacities to enable a online content curation by users in the fight against fake news, and 2) the experimentation of the digitalization of Civil Registries, namely in the townships of Lacs 1 and Ogou 2 where a digital solutions was tested. 

9 different type of data sources per year 

As for data types used, structured and unstructured as well spatiotemporal data were exploited in the exploration of issues related to 1) the improvement of the livelihoods of the borderland communities of Aflao and Hillacondji, 2) the conservation of fruits and vegetables locally, 3) the farming of Black Soldier Fly larvae. 

50 new potential solutions per lab per year 

During the exploration of fruits and vegetables conservation methods, 7 grassroots solutions were mapped. 11 more were discovered during the visits to the Energy Generations - a local incubator &#x2013; compound, though it must be noted that these solutions were IPs of local startups. Furthermore, the exploration leading to the experimentation with Black Soldier Flies has highlighted different uses of larvae from directly feeding poultry to mixing its powder into animal food. Three solutions were found at the exploration of the use of digital solution to improve the science teaching at the secondary schools. 

Some of the new potential solutions that have been found are: avocado based soap; avocado based oil; black soldier flies farming, use of videos at secondary school, Tontine as cofounding solution to finance business, coconut supply chain, cassava supply chain, kente weaving (local wrapper in Togo, Ghana and Cote d&#x2019;Ivoire), alternative route to cross borders, market sitted on the no-mans-land  cross Benin and Togo; 

10 innovation methods per Action Plan. 

When it comes to innovation methods used, design thinking and collective intelligence were used throughout the work with the borderlands communities to understand their behaviors in cross border trading and their attitude towards finding the most effective solutions to better the livelihoods of Kente weavers. The following methods were used with the different stakeholders of secondary education: brainstorming, cocreation, and focus group. To understand the value chains of fruits and vegetable, the team used immersion, ethnography, interview as methods 

At least 8 blogs, or other (substantial) media posts published per year. 

During the year, two blogs have been effectively published, describing the work with the Healthy Internet Project. However, 2 more were submitted but never approved. 

https://www.undp.org/fr/togo/blog/covid-19-des-conversations-radio-pour-influer-les-politiques-de-developpement 

https://www.undp.org/fr/togo/blog/lutter-contre-la-fausse-information-directement-de-son-navigateur-cest-possible 

At least 50% of our learning challenges have the public sector or other partner as counterpart. 

The majority, even more than 60% of the partners of different experimentations are from public or private sector or local communities. During the exploration, solution mapping and experimentation phases of Kente weaving at Agbozume, local trading communities from the Togolese and Ghanian sides of the border have been associated in the identification of the value chain to focus on, and then during experimentation, the local Kente association was heavily involved in the design of the solutions to implement. Also, on that project, the Ghana Enterprise Agency, a government entity, was a key partner in community management and grassroots participation. To experiment the farming of Black Soldier Flies, the weather early forecasting to allow farmers to anticipate on early preparation of their fields, online crowdfunding system, private sector enterprises were engaged.</narrative>
